# Lobbying Certification and Disclosure Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Article I — Purpose

This policy ensures that {{ org_name }} ("the Organization") complies with the Byrd Anti-Lobbying Amendment (31 USC 1352), OMB cost principles governing lobbying (2 CFR 200.450), and the lobbying restrictions applicable to organizations exempt under IRC Section 501(c)(3). It applies to all directors, officers, employees, and agents of the Organization.

## Article II — Prohibition on Use of Federal Funds for Lobbying

No federal funds received by the Organization — whether through grants, cooperative agreements, contracts, or loans — may be used to pay any person for influencing or attempting to influence an officer or employee of any federal agency, a Member of Congress, an officer or employee of Congress, or an employee of a Member of Congress in connection with the awarding, making, entering into, extension, continuation, renewal, amendment, or modification of any federal award.

## Article III — 501(c)(3) Lobbying Restrictions

As a 501(c)(3) organization, and consistent with Article 12 of its Articles of Incorporation, the Organization shall not engage in substantial lobbying activity or intervene in any political campaign on behalf of or in opposition to any candidate for public office. The Organization has not made the Section 501(h) election; therefore any lobbying activity must remain insubstantial under the facts-and-circumstances test.

## Article IV — Certification Requirement

For every federal award, contract, grant, cooperative agreement, or loan exceeding **$100,000**, the Executive Director shall execute and submit a certification (Standard Form LLL or equivalent form required by the awarding agency) affirming that no appropriated federal funds have been paid or will be paid for lobbying activities in connection with the award. This certification must be submitted at the time of application or bid and updated whenever the facts change.

## Article V — Disclosure of Non-Federal Lobbying

If the Organization uses **non-federal funds** to pay for lobbying activities related to a federal award that requires certification under Article IV, the Organization shall file **Standard Form LLL** (Disclosure of Lobbying Activities) with the awarding agency. The disclosure must identify the lobbying registrant, the individual performing the lobbying, the award involved, and the amount expended.

## Article VI — Definition of Lobbying

For purposes of this policy, **lobbying** means any oral or written communication with a covered federal official — including Members of Congress, congressional staff, and officers or employees of federal agencies — that is intended to influence decisions regarding federal awards, contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, or loans.

### Permitted Activities

The following are **not** lobbying under this policy:

- Providing technical or factual information in response to a documented request from a federal official;
- Responding to published solicitations for proposals, applications, or bids;
- Routine grant management communications (progress reports, budget negotiations, award administration); and
- Communications required by statute, regulation, or the terms of an award.

## Article VII — Record Keeping

The Organization shall maintain copies of all lobbying certifications, SF-LLL disclosures, and supporting documentation for a minimum of **three years** from the date of submission of the final expenditure report for the applicable federal award, consistent with 2 CFR 200.334 and the Organization's Document Retention Policy.

## Article VIII — Subrecipient and Contractor Flow-Down

All subaward agreements and contracts exceeding **$100,000** under a federal award shall include a requirement that the subrecipient or contractor submit its own lobbying certification and, where applicable, file Standard Form LLL. The Organization shall collect and retain these certifications as part of the subaward or contract file.

## Article IX — Violations and Penalties

Any person who makes a prohibited expenditure or fails to file or amend a required certification or disclosure is subject to a **civil penalty of not less than $10,000 and not more than $100,000** for each such failure, as provided by 31 USC 1352(d). Violations may also result in disciplinary action, termination of the federal award, and debarment from future federal funding.

## Article X — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy annually. The Executive Director shall confirm that all required certifications and disclosures have been filed and that no violations have occurred. This review may take place at any regular or special board meeting and shall be noted in the meeting minutes.
